===============================================================
27.1 MODULAR PROGRAMMING
===============================================================

Modular programming means dividing a large program into
smaller, logically independent modules.

Instead of putting everything into one huge file:

    main()
    function1()
    function2()
    function3()
    function4()
    ...
    function100()

we divide the program according to responsibilities.

For example:

    Student Management System

        main.c
        student.c
        student.h
        file.c
        file.h
        menu.c
        menu.h


Each module handles a specific responsibility.


---------------------------------------------------------------
WHY MODULAR PROGRAMMING?
---------------------------------------------------------------

Advantages:

    1. Better organization
    2. Code reusability
    3. Easier debugging
    4. Easier testing
    5. Easier maintenance
    6. Team development
    7. Reduced complexity
    8. Better separation of responsibilities


---------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
---------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of:

    main.c

containing:

    input code
    student logic
    file handling
    searching
    sorting
    menu
    validation

we can organize:

    main.c
        ↓
    menu.c
        ↓
    student.c
        ↓
    file.c


Each component has a clear responsibility.


===============================================================
27.2 HEADER FILES
===============================================================

A header file normally contains declarations and interfaces
that can be shared between source files.

Typical extension:

    .h

Example:

    student.h


---------------------------------------------------------------
student.h
---------------------------------------------------------------

    #ifndef STUDENT_H
    #define STUDENT_H

    typedef struct
    {
        int id;
        char name[50];
        float marks;
    } Student;

    void add_student(Student *s);
    void print_student(const Student *s);

    #endif


The header tells other source files:

    "These types/functions are available."


---------------------------------------------------------------
SOURCE FILE
---------------------------------------------------------------

student.c

    #include "student.h"

    void add_student(Student *s)
    {
        /* implementation */
    }

    void print_student(const Student *s)
    {
        /* implementation */
    }


---------------------------------------------------------------
MAIN FILE
---------------------------------------------------------------

main.c

    #include "student.h"

    int main(void)
    {
        Student s;

        add_student(&s);
        print_student(&s);

        return 0;
    }


The relationship is:

                    student.h
                   /         \
                  /           \
             student.c       main.c
                  \           /
                   \         /
                    compiler
                       ↓
                 object files
                       ↓
                     linker
                       ↓
                   executable


===============================================================
DECLARATION VS DEFINITION
===============================================================

This distinction is extremely important in modular C.

DECLARATION:

    Tells the compiler that something exists.

Example:

    int add(int a, int b);


DEFINITION:

    Provides the actual implementation.

Example:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }


A header commonly contains declarations.

A source file commonly contains definitions.


---------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
---------------------------------------------------------------

math_utils.h:

    int add(int a, int b);
    int multiply(int a, int b);


math_utils.c:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }

    int multiply(int a, int b)
    {
        return a * b;
    }


main.c:

    #include "math_utils.h"

    int main(void)
    {
        int x = add(10, 20);
        int y = multiply(5, 6);

        return 0;
    }


===============================================================
27.3 SOURCE FILES
===============================================================

C source files normally use:

    .c

Example project:

    project/
    |
    +-- main.c
    |
    +-- math.c
    +-- math.h
    |
    +-- student.c
    +-- student.h
    |
    +-- file.c
    +-- file.h


Each .c file can contain implementations for a particular
module.


---------------------------------------------------------------
GOOD SOURCE FILE DESIGN
---------------------------------------------------------------

math.c

    #include "math.h"

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }

    int subtract(int a, int b)
    {
        return a - b;
    }


math.h

    #ifndef MATH_H
    #define MATH_H

    int add(int a, int b);
    int subtract(int a, int b);

    #endif


This gives a clean interface:

    math.h
       ↓
    What is available?

    math.c
       ↓
    How is it implemented?


===============================================================
27.4 FUNCTION ORGANIZATION
===============================================================

Functions should be organized according to responsibility.

BAD DESIGN:

    main.c

    main()
    addStudent()
    deleteStudent()
    saveFile()
    loadFile()
    printMenu()
    searchStudent()
    sortStudent()
    validateInput()
    ...

Everything becomes tightly connected.


GOOD DESIGN:

    main.c
        |
        +-- menu.c
        |
        +-- student.c
        |
        +-- storage.c
        |
        +-- validation.c


---------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
---------------------------------------------------------------

Student application:

    student.h
        Student data type
        add_student()
        delete_student()
        search_student()

    student.c
        implementation of student operations

    storage.h
        save_students()
        load_students()

    storage.c
        file handling implementation

    menu.h
        display_menu()

    menu.c
        menu implementation

    main.c
        program entry point


Each module has a focused responsibility.


===============================================================
27.5 SEPARATE COMPILATION
===============================================================

One of the most important professional C concepts.

A large C program does NOT have to be compiled as one giant
source file.

Instead, each source file can be compiled separately.

Suppose:

    main.c
    math.c
    student.c


Compilation:

    main.c
       ↓
    main.o

    math.c
       ↓
    math.o

    student.c
       ↓
    student.o


Then the linker combines the object files:

    main.o
       \
    math.o -----> LINKER -----> executable
       /
    student.o


---------------------------------------------------------------
COMMANDS
---------------------------------------------------------------

Compile only:

    gcc -c main.c

Produces:

    main.o


Compile:

    gcc -c math.c

Produces:

    math.o


Compile:

    gcc -c student.c

Produces:

    student.o


Link:

    gcc main.o math.o student.o -o program


Run:

    ./program


---------------------------------------------------------------
WHY SEPARATE COMPILATION?
---------------------------------------------------------------

Imagine a project contains:

    100 source files.

You change only:

    student.c

You ideally do not need to recompile every unchanged source
file.

Only the affected module can be recompiled, then the linker
can rebuild the executable.

This can significantly improve build organization and time.


===============================================================
27.6 STATIC LIBRARIES
===============================================================

A static library is a collection of compiled object code that
can be linked into an executable.

Common Unix-like naming convention:

    libsomething.a


Example:

    libmath.a


Suppose we have:

    math.c

with:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }


Compile:

    gcc -c math.c


Produces:

    math.o


Create a static library using an archive tool:

    ar rcs libmath.a math.o


Now:

    libmath.a

contains the object code.


---------------------------------------------------------------
USING THE LIBRARY
---------------------------------------------------------------

main.c:

    #include "math.h"

    int main(void)
    {
        return add(10, 20);
    }


Compile/link:

    gcc main.c -L. -lmath -o program


Meaning:

    -L.
        Search current directory for libraries.

    -lmath
        Link with the math library named approximately:

        libmath.a


---------------------------------------------------------------
STATIC LINKING CONCEPT
---------------------------------------------------------------

Conceptually:

    main.o
       +
    library object code
       |
       v
    executable


The required library code becomes part of the resulting
executable according to the linking process.


===============================================================
27.7 SHARED LIBRARIES — OVERVIEW
===============================================================

A shared library is designed to be used dynamically.

Common Unix-like naming convention:

    .so

Example:

    libmath.so


Windows commonly uses:

    .dll


macOS commonly uses:

    .dylib


The exact mechanisms vary by operating system and toolchain.


---------------------------------------------------------------
STATIC VS SHARED
---------------------------------------------------------------

STATIC:

    program
       |
       +-- library code linked into executable


SHARED:

    program -----> shared library


Conceptually, shared libraries can allow multiple programs to
use common library code rather than each executable containing
its own copy.


---------------------------------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES OF SHARED LIBRARIES
---------------------------------------------------------------

    • Can reduce duplicated library code
    • Libraries can be updated independently in some setups
    • Multiple programs can share library code
    • Useful for large software systems


Potential complexities include:

    • runtime library availability
    • version compatibility
    • loader/search-path issues
    • ABI compatibility


===============================================================
STATIC LIBRARY VS SHARED LIBRARY
===============================================================

+------------------+-----------------------------+
| Static           | Shared                      |
+------------------+-----------------------------+
| Linked into      | Loaded/used dynamically     |
| executable       | according to platform       |
|                  | mechanisms                  |
| Often .a         | Often .so on Unix-like OS   |
| Library code     | Library may be shared       |
| becomes part of  | among programs              |
| executable       |                             |
+------------------+-----------------------------+

The exact behavior is platform/toolchain dependent.


===============================================================
27.8 MAKEFILES
===============================================================

When a project contains many files, manually typing:

    gcc -c main.c
    gcc -c math.c
    gcc -c student.c
    gcc main.o math.o student.o -o program

becomes inconvenient.

A Makefile automates the build process.

Typical command:

    make


---------------------------------------------------------------
SIMPLE MAKEFILE
---------------------------------------------------------------

    CC = gcc
    CFLAGS = -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic

    program: main.o math.o
        $(CC) main.o math.o -o program

    main.o: main.c math.h
        $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c main.c

    math.o: math.c math.h
        $(CC) $(CFLAGS) -c math.c

    clean:
        rm -f *.o program


IMPORTANT:

    In a traditional Makefile, the command lines beneath
    targets are conventionally indented with a TAB.


---------------------------------------------------------------
MAKEFILE STRUCTURE
---------------------------------------------------------------

    target: dependencies
        command


Example:

    program: main.o math.o
        gcc main.o math.o -o program


Meaning:

    Target:
        program

    Dependencies:
        main.o
        math.o

    Command:
        gcc main.o math.o -o program


===============================================================
27.9 BUILD PROCESS
===============================================================

A professional C build involves several stages.

SOURCE CODE
    |
    v
PREPROCESSING
    |
    v
COMPILATION
    |
    v
ASSEMBLY
    |
    v
OBJECT FILE
    |
    v
LINKING
    |
    v
EXECUTABLE


---------------------------------------------------------------
1. PREPROCESSING
---------------------------------------------------------------

Processes directives such as:

    #include
    #define
    #if
    #ifdef


Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

The preprocessor processes the inclusion according to the
language/toolchain rules.


---------------------------------------------------------------
2. COMPILATION
---------------------------------------------------------------

The compiler translates C source into lower-level
representation and ultimately assembly/object code as part
of the toolchain.


---------------------------------------------------------------
3. ASSEMBLY
---------------------------------------------------------------

Assembly is converted into machine-level object code.

Output is commonly:

    .o

on Unix-like systems.


---------------------------------------------------------------
4. LINKING
---------------------------------------------------------------

The linker combines object files and resolves references
between them.

Example:

    main.o
       |
       +-------> add()
                  ^
                  |
               math.o


The linker connects the call in main.o to the definition
provided by math.o.


---------------------------------------------------------------
5. EXECUTABLE
---------------------------------------------------------------

After successful linking:

    program

can be executed by the operating system.


===============================================================
COMPLETE PROFESSIONAL PROJECT EXAMPLE
===============================================================

Directory:

    student_app/
    |
    +-- main.c
    |
    +-- student.c
    +-- student.h
    |
    +-- storage.c
    +-- storage.h
    |
    +-- Makefile


---------------------------------------------------------------
student.h
---------------------------------------------------------------

    #ifndef STUDENT_H
    #define STUDENT_H

    typedef struct
    {
        int id;
        char name[50];
    } Student;

    void print_student(const Student *s);

    #endif


---------------------------------------------------------------
student.c
---------------------------------------------------------------

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include "student.h"

    void print_student(const Student *s)
    {
        if (s == NULL)
            return;

        printf("ID: %d\n", s->id);
        printf("Name: %s\n", s->name);
    }


---------------------------------------------------------------
main.c
---------------------------------------------------------------

    #include "student.h"

    int main(void)
    {
        Student s = {1, "Apoorv"};

        print_student(&s);

        return 0;
    }


---------------------------------------------------------------
BUILD
---------------------------------------------------------------

    gcc -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -c student.c
    gcc -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -c main.c

    gcc main.o student.o -o student_app


Run:

    ./student_app


This is a basic example of modular C programming.


===============================================================
27.10 CODE QUALITY
===============================================================

The syllabus identifies five important areas:

    1. Naming conventions
    2. Comments
    3. Formatting
    4. Error handling
    5. Maintainability


---------------------------------------------------------------
A. NAMING CONVENTIONS
---------------------------------------------------------------

Names should communicate purpose.

BAD:

    int x;
    int y;
    int z;

GOOD:

    int student_count;
    int total_marks;
    int max_value;


Function:

BAD:

    void f(int x);

GOOD:

    void print_student(const Student *student);


Good naming reduces the need for comments.


---------------------------------------------------------------
B. COMMENTS
---------------------------------------------------------------

Comments should explain intent or non-obvious decisions.

Poor:

    x++;   // increment x


This tells us what the code literally does.

More useful:

    // Skip the header row before processing records.
    row++;


Do not write huge comments explaining obvious code.

Code should be readable by itself wherever practical.


---------------------------------------------------------------
C. FORMATTING
---------------------------------------------------------------

Consistent formatting improves readability.

Example:

    if (student_count > 0)
    {
        print_students(students, student_count);
    }


Maintain consistency in:

    • indentation
    • braces
    • spacing
    • naming
    • function layout


The exact formatting convention can vary between projects.


---------------------------------------------------------------
D. ERROR HANDLING
---------------------------------------------------------------

Professional programs should handle failures.

Example:

    FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "r");

    if (fp == NULL)
    {
        perror("data.txt");
        return 1;
    }


Instead of assuming:

    fopen()

always succeeds.

Similarly, check operations whose failure matters:

    malloc()
    fopen()
    fread()
    fwrite()
    command-line arguments
    user input
    etc.


---------------------------------------------------------------
E. MAINTAINABILITY
---------------------------------------------------------------

Maintainable code should be:

    • understandable
    • modular
    • testable
    • reasonably documented
    • consistently formatted
    • resistant to unnecessary duplication


A useful design principle is:

    One module
        →
    clear responsibility


Avoid creating functions that are enormous and perform many
unrelated tasks.


===============================================================
HEADER GUARDS
===============================================================

When a header is included multiple times, declarations may
otherwise be processed repeatedly.

Traditional header guards:

    #ifndef STUDENT_H
    #define STUDENT_H

    /* declarations */

    #endif


This ensures the contents are processed only once per
translation unit under the guard macro.


---------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE
---------------------------------------------------------------

student.h:

    #ifndef STUDENT_H
    #define STUDENT_H

    typedef struct
    {
        int id;
    } Student;

    void print_student(const Student *s);

    #endif


This is especially important in larger modular projects.


===============================================================
DECLARATION → DEFINITION → LINKING
===============================================================

A very important professional C chain:

    HEADER
      |
      | declaration
      v
    main.c
      |
      | function call
      v
    main.o
      |
      | linker resolves symbol
      v
    module.o
      |
      | definition
      v
    executable


Example:

math.h:

    int add(int, int);


main.c:

    #include "math.h"

    int main(void)
    {
        return add(2, 3);
    }


math.c:

    int add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }


Compilation creates:

    main.o
    math.o


Linking connects:

    main.o
       |
       | add()
       v
    math.o


===============================================================
COMMON MISTAKES
===============================================================

1. Putting every function into main.c.

2. Including a .c file instead of its header.

Usually:

    #include "student.h"

not:

    #include "student.c"


3. Defining the same external function in multiple source
   files accidentally.

4. Forgetting to link an object file.

5. Forgetting a required function declaration.

6. Creating circular or poorly designed module dependencies.

7. Not checking errors.

8. Inconsistent naming and formatting.

9. Writing extremely large functions.

10. Not using dependency-aware builds for larger projects.


===============================================================
IMPORTANT COMPILATION ERROR VS LINKER ERROR
===============================================================

Suppose:

main.c:

    int add(int, int);

    int main(void)
    {
        return add(2, 3);
    }


If we compile:

    gcc -c main.c

Compilation can succeed because the compiler knows:

    int add(int, int);


But if we then link without providing the definition:

    gcc main.o -o program


the linker may report an undefined reference to add.

This illustrates:

    Declaration available
            ↓
       compilation OK
            ↓
    Definition missing
            ↓
       linking fails


===============================================================
GATE / INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
===============================================================

Q1. Why use header files?

Answer:

    To provide shared declarations/interfaces and types that
    can be included by source files.


Q2. What is separate compilation?

Answer:

    Compiling source files independently into object files and
    then linking those object files into the final program.


Q3. What is a static library?

Answer:

    A library containing object code that can be linked into
    an executable.


Q4. What is a shared library?

Answer:

    A library intended for dynamic/shared use according to
    the platform's runtime and linking mechanisms.


Q5. What does a Makefile do?

Answer:

    It describes build targets, dependencies, and commands so
    the build process can be automated.


Q6. What is the difference between a declaration and a
definition?

Answer:

    Declaration tells the compiler about an entity.

    Definition provides the entity's implementation/storage
    as applicable.


Q7. Why is modular programming useful?

Answer:

    It improves organization, reuse, debugging, testing and
    maintainability.


Q8. What is separate compilation useful for?

Answer:

    Large projects can be organized into independently
    compiled modules, reducing unnecessary recompilation and
    improving build organization.


Q9. What is the general C build pipeline?

Answer:

    Preprocessing
        ↓
    Compilation
        ↓
    Assembly
        ↓
    Linking
        ↓
    Executable


===============================================================
MASTER REVISION
===============================================================

                 PROFESSIONAL C PROJECT

                         |
              +----------+----------+
              |                     |
          HEADER FILES          SOURCE FILES
              |                     |
        declarations           definitions
              |                     |
              +----------+----------+
                         |
                SEPARATE COMPILATION
                         |
                     .o files
                         |
                       LINKER
                         |
             +-----------+-----------+
             |                       |
          STATIC                  SHARED
         LIBRARY                 LIBRARY
             |                       |
             +-----------+-----------+
                         |
                    EXECUTABLE


Build automation:

    Makefile
       ↓
    dependencies
       ↓
    compile only what is needed
       ↓
    link
       ↓
    executable


Code quality:

    Naming
       +
    Comments
       +
    Formatting
       +
    Error handling
       +
    Maintainability
       =
    Professional C code


===============================================================
CHAPTER 27 — FINAL TAKEAWAY
===============================================================

Beginner C:

    one file
    ↓
    compile
    ↓
    run


Professional C:

    multiple modules
        ↓
    header files
        ↓
    source files
        ↓
    separate compilation
        ↓
    object files
        ↓
    libraries
        ↓
    linker
        ↓
    Makefile/build system
        ↓
    executable


The key idea:

    GOOD C PROGRAMMING IS NOT ONLY ABOUT
    WRITING CORRECT CODE.

    It is also about organizing that code so that
    it remains understandable, reusable, testable,
    buildable and maintainable.

